+#### Long-running Sessions
+
+An MCP client keeps one `drush mcp:server` process alive for the whole session. Before each message, the server resets that process to the state a new web request starts in:
+
+- Services tagged `needs_destruction` are destructed, as at the end of a web request.
+- In-memory copies of state, config, cache tag checksums, cache collectors and memory cache bins are dropped, including loaded entities.
+- Plugin managers reload their definitions from the discovery cache, so plugins rebuilt by a `drush cr` elsewhere show up. Entity type definitions are not reloaded.
+- `drupal_static()` is reset and the request time moves to now.
+
+Writes made by other processes, such as the admin UI, Drush or cron, therefore show up on the next message.
+
+A service that keeps its own data in memory can opt in: tag it `mcp_server.request_scoped` and implement `Symfony\Contracts\Service\ResetInterface`. Its `reset()` method then runs before each message.
+
+```yaml
+services:
+  my_module.lookup:
+    class: Drupal\my_module\Lookup
+    tags:
+      - { name: mcp_server.request_scoped }
+```
+
+The container, entity type definitions, path alias lookups, tool access and the tool, resource and prompt lists are still fixed when the process starts. Restart the process after installing modules, changing code, or changing permissions or MCP configuration.
+
 #### Manual Testing
 
 You can test the STDIO server manually:
